---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import d from "../data/bristol-chapels.json";
const win = new Set(d.window);
const byPlace = [...new Set(d.rows.map((r) => r.place))];
---
<Base
  title="The Bristol chapels"
  eyebrow={`RG 4 · ${d.total} registers · catalogued ${d.searched}`}
  dek="The one open question in this archive's English line is where Hannah Saniger was born. The likeliest answer is a Bristol chapel. Here is every Bristol chapel register the state holds, and exactly which ones could contain her.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">When the Registrar General took in the nonconformist registers in 1837 they were
    bound, numbered and shelved as <strong>RG 4</strong>. They are the registers of people who did
    not christen their children in the parish church — Baptists, Presbyterians, Wesleyans,
    Moravians, Welsh Calvinistic Methodists — and they are exactly where a Bristol
    working family of the 1810s is most likely to be found.</p>

    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem">This archive's errand list has said for weeks that there were
    <em>five</em> Bristol chapel registers to read. That was a guess, and it was wrong by a factor of
    seven. The catalogue holds <strong>{d.total}</strong>.</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>Twelve of them can contain a birth between 1805 and 1815</strong> — the window for
      {" "}<a href={u("/people/hannah-saniger")}>Hannah Saniger</a>. The other twenty-four cannot,
      and can now be struck off without opening them. That is the point of doing this before
      travelling anywhere.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Every Bristol register in RG 4</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">{d.total} volumes, {byPlace.length} chapels</h2>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th class="m">Piece</th><th class="m">Covers</th><th>Chapel</th><th>Denomination</th><th>Contents</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {d.rows.map((r) => (
            <tr style={win.has(r.ref) ? "background:var(--tint)" : ""}>
              <td class="mono small">{win.has(r.ref) ? <strong>{r.ref}</strong> : r.ref}</td>
              <td class="m">{r.from}–{r.to}</td>
              <td>{win.has(r.ref) ? <strong>{r.place}</strong> : r.place}</td>
              <td class="small">{r.den}</td>
              <td class="small">{r.kind}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:.9rem">Shaded rows are births or baptisms whose dates cover
    1805–1815. <span class="chip doc">catalogue, verbatim</span></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">One detail worth keeping</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Bristol is filed under Somerset</h2>

    <p class="lede">Nearly every one of these registers is catalogued
    <strong>"Bristol, … , Somerset"</strong>. Only Bridge Street Chapel is filed under Gloucester.
    The city straddles the Avon and therefore the county line, and clerks have been choosing sides
    ever since.</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>This matters here more than it looks.</strong> The
      <a href={u("/drivers-fields")}>1841 census at Drivers Fields</a> records Hannah as
      <em>not</em> born in Gloucestershire — a single tick in a column. If she was born south of the
      Avon in what everyone including her called Bristol, that tick is correct, and it has been read
      for years as though it pointed away from the city. It does not. It points across a river.
      <a href={u("/sanigers")}>Where the search has already been →</a>
      <span class="chip inf">an argument, not a finding</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>What to do with this</h2>
    <ol class="dek">
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Twelve volumes, named and numbered.</strong> They are
      digitised, and can be read without leaving the house. This is now a specific task rather than
      an aspiration. <a href={u("/errands")}>On the queue →</a></li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Broad Mead and Lewins Mead first.</strong> Broad Mead
      is the great Bristol Baptist congregation and its births run 1787–1817 in one volume
      (RG 4/2697); Lewins Mead's Presbyterian baptisms run 1718–1840 in another (RG 4/1830). Between
      them they cover the whole window twice over.</li>
      <li><strong>And the honest caveat.</strong> Nothing yet suggests the Sanigers were
      nonconformist. This is where to look because it is where the Anglican registers are not, not
      because there is evidence pointing at a chapel. <span class="chip inf">a hypothesis being
      tested</span></li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</Base>
